<h4>The challenge of Web and Software localisation -- a lot more than just translation</h4>

<h3>Web localisation</H3>
<p>You want to sell your brand. You want to reach new clients.</p>

<p>Whether your brand is a set of services or products, you want your online presence to grab a potential client’s attention. Your key market may be in Germany, but you may be seeking to expand into the English-speaking market. Or you may be seeking to attract international clients here in Germany.</p> 

<p>That’s why you want your website to be translated into one or more target languages. And you want it to read like the original text, not like a translation. </p>

<p>Language Landscapes knows that the translation of your text has a unique purpose – to communicate your brands to new clients in a way that they can relate to and understand. </p>

<p>Translating your text, however, is only one aspect of what we do. In order to strike the right chord with your target audience, we need to ensure that your brand identity also translates correspondingly. To do this, communicating with you on a regular basis throughout the process is vital. It also means we can develop with you a glossary of company-specific and/or product-specific terminology for consistency.</p>

<p>As part of our quality control process, we check that the translated content fits within your existing site design, returning your website to you in the same format as the original, but in a different language. We also take care to ensure that all links are intact, tags are preserved and keywords localised optimally.</p>

<H3>Software localisation</H3>

<p>There are not many software development companies these days who still do their development work using a single, simple architecture. Most modern software development projects involve a mixture of platforms, paradigms and frameworks mixed and matched in the race to produce the range of functionality and quality of presentation required by today's software users and to get new versions of software out before the competition.  Nor can many such companies limit themselves to one particular cultural market when the time comes get selling their products worldwide.  The time is long gone where software consumers are happy to have functionality at the price of a difficult user interface that won't speak the user's language.</p>

<p>What this means in practice is on the one hand that the demand for software localisation services into multiple languages has exploded over the last few years and that, on the other, the task of localisation has become more and more complex as programs and software development environments have become ever more complex and dynamic and consumer demand has got ever more sophisticated.</p>

<p>At Language Landscapes, we can provide you with a complete solution tailor-made for your product or service and for your development environment.</p>

<h4>Localisation</h4>
<h4>What is localisation anyway?</h4>
<p>The classical definition of localisation is the adaptation of content (whether it be web or print content, or even the materials that appear on graphical user interface of a software application) to the cultural expectations of the target consumer of the content.</p>
<p>In reality, this task is a fairly complex one. It involves a wide range of different disciplines.</p>

<h4>Formatting</h4>
<p>It can affect symbols that appear on a communication ; the way German or Spanish web users expect to see currency amounts (99,- € would be acceptable, for example) differ from what English-speaking users are comfortable with (€ 99.00 would look good to them). To take another example, Hungarian dates look very different to German, Spanish or English ones (which also differ substantially).</p>

<h4>Programming</h4>
<p>It might even involve changes in programming logic:</p> 
<p>For example, Spanish customers will appreciate being allowed to sign onto a mailing list using at least two surnames (they can have up to four) and some Germans will be pleasantly surprised if they’re addressed on mailings sent to them using their full honorific and title (Professor Dr Pimpelhuber smiles whenever he sees his full name correctly printed), whereas many Brits prefer to be on first name terms with the people they do business with.</p>
<p>Pleasing everyone means more complex programming and expense.  Localisation decisions aim to limit that extra expense to functions that actually bring added value.  And decision will be different, depending on whether you’re running an online garden centre or a text centre for medical devices.</p>

<h4>Sizing and text directionality</h4>
<p>Some terms in the original that fit into the space reserved for them in dialogs and webpages may not fit in translation.  This often means that the dimensions of elements within may need to be adjusted to fit the text they contain. In some language combinations the size of an entire dialog may need to be adjusted.</p>
<p>Translations into non-European languages (which are presented in the familiar left-to-right directionality) may require dialogs to run from right to left (as with Hebrew and Arabic) or even top to bottom (as with Chinese and other Far Eastern languages).</p>

<h4>Content</h4>
<p>Sometimes content needs to be changed to suit the interests of readers in the target culture.  A manufacturer in Central Scotland might, for example, have a page proudly proclaiming the impact of her company on employment in East Lanarkshire that is unlikely to be of much interest to a customer in German Saarland.  Localisers will discuss with webmasters whether to replace this with something more suitable for their German website visitors or simply delete it.</p>
<p>The references people will want to see, the links they’ll find interesting and even the pictures they’ll find attractive will change from culture to culture and from language to language.</p>

<h4>Communicative translation</h4>
<p>Marketing language can be more effusive in some languages than in others.  German companies are proud to splash all over their homepage the fact that the famous Stiftung Warentest has tested their product and given it the accolade “Gut” and prospective customers all over the country are suitably impressed. However, the English equivalent of the word (“Good”) sets no hearts racing.  Even “excellent” can sound tame in British marketing blurb.</p>
<p>As a result, translations of English texts into German can sound breathless and hyperbolic to German site visitors, while translations in the opposite direction can often seem a little dull and uninspiring. The tone and sometimes even the meaning carried by translations may often need to be adjusted to take account of this fact.</p>

<h4>Different cultures, different expectations</h4>
<p>Another surprising fact is that the tolerance of incomplete or unpolished localisation differs from culture to culture.  German or Spanish web surfers are well used to English texts popping up intermittently on buttons, side bars and menus and to lazy translations into their native language that follow English grammar conventions. The same is not true in reverse.</p>

<p>Many English-speaking consumers will react to isolated German or Spanish texts on otherwise English-language sites with incomprehension and slight irritation, while German idioms transliterated into English can often produce unintended hilarity.  Several decades of incomplete or literal translations has had its influence on German expectations, and sometimes even on the German language itself (think, for example, of how the English construction “xy macht Sinn” ("xy makes sense") is fast replacing the older German “x ist sinvoll” or of how German internet users no longer bat an eyelid when they see the word “Download” on a button.  The appearance of “Herunterladen” or even the classic Deunglish "Downloaden" on an English website, however, would not go unnoticed, and would not appear professional.</p>

<h4>Cultural “bumpiness”</h4>
<p>This text was written originally in English.  If the German version is seems a little unnatural, with little tics that give away that it's wasn't originally written by a completely fluent English speaker, then we haven't done our job as well as we'd like. Our aim is to free your text of any traces of the linguistic culture of the source language that may distract from your branding message.  The task can mean anything from making slight changes in punctuation to completely reworking texts.</p>

